ically June 1950 falls in FY 1950, the U.S. government might recognize the conflict differently when tracking budgetary expenses.\n\nI'll analyze the transfer data from 1947, noting the initial transfer was minimal at 0.4 million dollars. The progression shows a gradual increase in transfers, with significant jumps in subsequent years.\n\nMy calculation will focus on determining the compound annual growth rate between these fiscal years, considering the nuanced timing of war-related expenditures.\n\nI'll carefully map out the fiscal year periods, ensuring I capture the precise start and end points for an accurate CAGR computation. The goal is to understand the financial trajectory during this critical historical period.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_84341d748cea47f6af3173f7","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"grep -i \"korean war\" /app/resources/*.txt"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
